ARTICLES=[
 dict(slug='what-to-automate-first',category='Intelligence',title='What should your business automate first?',desc='A practical way to identify repetitive work worth automating, without making a complicated process more complicated.',read='4 MIN READ',intro='The best first automation is usually a small, repeated task that has a clear input, a predictable result, and someone who knows what good looks like.',sections=[('Start with the work, not the tool.','Ask your team where information gets copied, where a handoff is regularly delayed, and which report takes the same effort every week. Observe a real run of that task. The exceptions you discover are often more useful than the ideal process diagram.'),('Look for a stable pattern.','A useful candidate happens often enough to matter and follows rules that can be written down. If the process changes every time, start by clarifying it. Automating ambiguity tends to move the confusion faster rather than resolve it.'),('Count the whole cost.','Consider time spent doing the task, checking it, correcting mistakes, and waiting for another person. Then consider the work required to build and maintain an integration. A small task that runs daily may be a better starting point than a complex task that happens twice a year.'),('Keep a person in the right place.','Decide which steps can run independently and which need review. An automation can prepare a message, organize information, or flag an exception without being allowed to make the final decision.'),('Define the failure path.','What happens when a tool is unavailable, a field is missing, or a request arrives twice? A trustworthy workflow needs visible status, a clear owner, and a recovery method. The first version is successful when someone can understand and operate it, not just when it completes a happy-path demo.')],related='automations'),
 dict(slug='a-brand-beyond-the-logo',category='Design',title='Your brand is bigger than its logo.',desc='How typography, color, imagery, and everyday decisions turn a visual identity into a consistent experience.',read='4 MIN READ',intro='A logo is a starting point. A brand becomes recognizable through a collection of decisions repeated with purpose.',sections=[('Begin with the character.','Before choosing a typeface or color, describe how the brand should feel and why. Precise words are useful: calm, direct, playful, meticulous. Generic words like premium need a more concrete definition before they can guide design.'),('Build relationships between elements.','Typography establishes voice. Color creates emphasis. Image direction sets a mood. Layout controls the rhythm of the message. These choices should work together, so the identity remains recognizable even when the logo is small or absent.'),('Test it in ordinary places.','A beautiful presentation is useful, but the real test is a proposal, a social post, a product label, or a mobile screen. Explore representative touchpoints early. An identity that only works at poster size is not yet a complete system.'),('Make the rules usable.','Guidelines should help people make decisions. Show examples of hierarchy, spacing, contrast, imagery, and common mistakes. Give the team templates for recurring needs so consistency does not depend on a designer rebuilding every asset.'),('Leave room for evolution.','A visual system should be coherent without becoming rigid. Separate the recognizable core from the flexible expressions around it. That gives campaigns room to feel fresh while still belonging to the same brand.')],related='branding'),
 dict(slug='from-storyboard-to-screen',category='Film',title='A stronger film starts before the first frame.',desc='Why a treatment, visual references, and a shot plan matter in both traditional and AI-assisted filmmaking.',read='5 MIN READ',intro='When production tools make images easier to create, decisions about story become even more important. A beautiful frame is only one part of a sequence.',sections=[('Know what changes in the scene.','Write down what the audience should understand or feel at the beginning and at the end. Every shot should help create that change. If the purpose is unclear, more visual detail rarely fixes the scene.'),('Give the world consistent rules.','Define the environment, characters, costumes, props, light, and point of view. References help collaborators make the same decisions repeatedly. In AI-assisted production, that shared reference set is especially important for continuity.'),('Plan the connection between shots.','A storyboard is not just a collection of attractive compositions. Screen direction, eyelines, distance, and movement should make the sequence understandable. Consider how the end of one shot creates the beginning of the next.'),('Let sound participate early.','Pacing can be shaped by a line of dialogue, a rhythm, an environmental sound, or silence. Rough sound in an early edit can reveal where a scene needs space or where a cut arrives too late.'),('Review a sequence, not just a frame.','Look for continuity, emotional progression, and whether the audience has enough information. A technically impressive shot can still interrupt the story. The final choice should serve the sequence.')],related='film-video'),
 dict(slug='designing-useful-ai',category='Intelligence',title='Useful AI begins with a smaller question.',desc='How a focused task, good information, and visible limits can make an AI feature more useful.',read='4 MIN READ',intro='“Add AI” is not yet a product brief. “Help our team find the right policy and show where it came from” is a much more useful beginning.',sections=[('Choose the job to be done.','Start with a real moment in someone’s work. What are they trying to understand, produce, or decide? Describe the current difficulty and the result that would help. This gives the team something concrete to design and evaluate.'),('Know the information behind the answer.','An assistant depends on the material it can access. Review relevance, freshness, permissions, and missing context. The quality of a response cannot be separated from the quality of the information and instructions behind it.'),('Make uncertainty visible.','An interface should not make every response look equally certain. Where appropriate, provide source references, explain limits, and offer a route to a person. Design the experience around the possibility of an incomplete or incorrect answer.'),('Test with the awkward examples.','Use realistic questions, ambiguous requests, unavailable information, and cases where the assistant should decline a task. A demonstration of the easiest case says little about how the system will behave in everyday work.'),('Measure usefulness, not novelty.','Look at whether users complete the task more effectively, whether the output needs correction, and whether the workflow fits their habits. Sometimes the best result is a small assistant that does one job clearly and predictably.')],related='ai-solutions')
]

from journal import NEW_ARTICLES
ARTICLES = NEW_ARTICLES + ARTICLES
import math
for article in ARTICLES:
 article['read'] = f"{max(2, math.ceil(len((' '.join(p for _,p in article['sections'])+' '+article['intro']).split()) / 220))} MIN READ"
